1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,385
Six laddoos cost a
hundred and twenty rupees.

2
00:00:03,385 --> 00:00:05,660
A packet of biscuits costs thirty.

3
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:06,860
Yet Mrs.

4
00:00:06,775 --> 00:00:09,759
Rao picks the laddoos, every single week.

5
00:00:09,759 --> 00:00:10,659
Why?

6
00:00:10,649 --> 00:00:15,017
Two ideas explain it: customer
value, and customer satisfaction.

7
00:00:15,617 --> 00:00:16,517
Mrs.

8
00:00:16,111 --> 00:00:19,697
Rao packs a tiffin for her
daughter every school morning.

9
00:00:19,997 --> 00:00:23,017
Each week, she quietly
asks herself two questions.

10
00:00:23,017 --> 00:00:24,142
Is it worth it?

11
00:00:24,142 --> 00:00:25,965
And did it keep its promise?

12
00:00:26,565 --> 00:00:28,594
First, what is customer value?

13
00:00:28,894 --> 00:00:31,981
Philip Kotler calls it
customer perceived value.

14
00:00:32,281 --> 00:00:36,487
It is the difference between all the
benefits a customer gets, and all

15
00:00:36,487 --> 00:00:39,554
the costs she pays,
compared with other choices.

16
00:00:39,854 --> 00:00:44,497
Perceived means it is what the customer
believes, not what the seller claims.

17
00:00:45,097 --> 00:00:45,997
Mrs.

18
00:00:45,609 --> 00:00:50,138
Rao's benefits: a healthy snack, a
taste her daughter loves, and trust

19
00:00:50,138 --> 00:00:51,421
in Nani's kitchen.

20
00:00:51,721 --> 00:00:55,997
Her costs: a hundred and twenty rupees,
the time to buy it, and a little

21
00:00:55,997 --> 00:00:56,897
worry.

22
00:00:56,835 --> 00:01:01,182
But Arjun delivers to her door, so
the time cost almost disappears.

23
00:01:01,482 --> 00:01:03,181
Biscuits cost less money.

24
00:01:03,181 --> 00:01:06,042
But for her, they give far fewer benefits.

25
00:01:06,642 --> 00:01:08,939
Customers don't buy the lowest price.

26
00:01:08,939 --> 00:01:10,607
They buy the most value.

27
00:01:11,307 --> 00:01:12,716
Next, satisfaction.

28
00:01:12,716 --> 00:01:16,934
Kotler describes it as pleasure or
disappointment, from comparing what

29
00:01:16,934 --> 00:01:19,078
you expected with what you got.

30
00:01:19,378 --> 00:01:22,094
Fall short, and the
customer is dissatisfied.

31
00:01:22,094 --> 00:01:24,126
Match it, and she is satisfied.

32
00:01:24,126 --> 00:01:26,114
Beat it, and she is delighted.

33
00:01:26,714 --> 00:01:28,028
Watch it happen.

34
00:01:28,028 --> 00:01:31,256
Arjun promises delivery
by seven every morning.

35
00:01:31,556 --> 00:01:32,456
Week one.

36
00:01:32,455 --> 00:01:36,184
He takes a wrong turn and
arrives at eight fifteen.

37
00:01:36,184 --> 00:01:37,084
Mrs.

38
00:01:36,743 --> 00:01:38,037
Rao is unhappy.

39
00:01:38,337 --> 00:01:39,237
Week two.

40
00:01:39,232 --> 00:01:41,482
He arrives at six fifty five.

41
00:01:41,482 --> 00:01:42,879
She is satisfied.

42
00:01:43,179 --> 00:01:44,079
Week three.

43
00:01:44,024 --> 00:01:48,743
On time again, and Nani tucks in a
handwritten note: made fresh this morning.

44
00:01:49,043 --> 00:01:49,943
Mrs.

45
00:01:49,513 --> 00:01:50,693
Rao is delighted.

46
00:01:50,693 --> 00:01:53,726
At the school gate, she
tells two other mothers.

47
00:01:54,326 --> 00:01:56,293
Where do we see this at work today?

48
00:01:56,593 --> 00:01:57,636
More benefits.

49
00:01:57,636 --> 00:02:01,097
A bank gives free money
advice, not just an account.

50
00:02:01,397 --> 00:02:02,372
Lower costs.

51
00:02:02,372 --> 00:02:05,893
A food delivery app saves
you time, not just money.

52
00:02:06,193 --> 00:02:07,643
Clear expectations.

53
00:02:07,643 --> 00:02:12,212
An airline shows the real arrival
time, so nobody is surprised.

54
00:02:12,512 --> 00:02:13,412
Delight.

55
00:02:13,263 --> 00:02:16,480
A hotel remembers that
you like a quiet room.

56
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:19,291
But should you always chase delight?

57
00:02:19,291 --> 00:02:20,191
Be careful.

58
00:02:20,435 --> 00:02:21,476
The good side.

59
00:02:21,476 --> 00:02:24,440
Delighted customers come
back, and bring friends.

60
00:02:24,740 --> 00:02:25,974
The hard side.

61
00:02:25,974 --> 00:02:29,881
Big promises raise expectations,
and extras cost money.

62
00:02:30,181 --> 00:02:33,423
Dev wants the poster to say,
delivered in ten minutes.

63
00:02:33,723 --> 00:02:34,732
Zoya says no.

64
00:02:34,732 --> 00:02:37,364
Promise seven, then beat it, every day.

65
00:02:37,664 --> 00:02:43,503
In 1984, Noriaki Kano showed that
today's delight slowly becomes tomorrow's

66
00:02:43,503 --> 00:02:44,708
basic need.

67
00:02:45,008 --> 00:02:45,908
The lesson?

68
00:02:45,782 --> 00:02:48,726
Promise what you can keep,
then give a little more.

69
00:02:49,326 --> 00:02:50,364
Quick check.

70
00:02:50,364 --> 00:02:51,948
Arjun promised seven.

71
00:02:51,948 --> 00:02:54,924
He arrives at six fifty five, as usual.

72
00:02:54,924 --> 00:02:56,080
How does Mrs.

73
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:56,980
Rao feel?

74
00:02:58,858 --> 00:02:59,758
Satisfied!

75
00:02:59,585 --> 00:03:01,940
What she got matched what she expected.

76
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:04,070
To delight her, he must beat it.

77
00:03:04,670 --> 00:03:05,679
So, remember.

78
00:03:05,979 --> 00:03:10,882
Customer value is all the benefits
minus all the costs, compared with other

79
00:03:10,882 --> 00:03:11,782
choices.

80
00:03:11,976 --> 00:03:15,484
Satisfaction compares what you
expected with what you got.

81
00:03:15,834 --> 00:03:20,167
Beat expectations honestly, and
customers come back, and bring friends.

82
00:03:20,767 --> 00:03:23,733
Next time, we'll explore
the marketing environment.
